  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 950 PM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nThe upper level trough will be closing off on Thursday as a\nsurface low deepens over the Carolinas. This will put us on the\nback side of the low and will bring unpleasant weather for \nThanksgiving. Cold air will be working into the region Thursday \nmorning keeping temperatures in the 40s into the afternoon. With \nthe increased pressure gradient we will see wind gusts 20-30 mph \nlate morning into Thursday night. The upper level low will be over\nus keeping overcast skies in place along with cold scattered \nshowers. As we head into the evening we will see temperatures fall\ninto the 30s and after 6 PM some location will be cold enough to \nsee some snow mix in with the showers. All of the area could see \nsome snow showers Thursday evening into Thursday night but showers\nwill linger the longest over the Plateau and they will see a \nlittle bit better forcing. There is a high chance for a dusting of\nsnow over the Plateau by Friday morning with a low chance (<20%) \nof snow accumulations up to 1\", snow accumulation is not expected\nelsewhere. Rain total with this system are looking to be between \n1 and 2 inches. \n\nLow chance for lingering showers Friday morning over the Plateau\nbut the upper level low will be lifting into the Northeast. This\nwill bring improving conditions with clearing skies. It will still\nbe cold with highs only in the mid 40s to lower 50s. Temperatures\nwarm back into the 50s and low 60s over the weekend as flow\nbecomes zonal. Models become uncertain with the pattern as we \nhead into next week with low chance for rain but overall dry \nconditions are favored with seasonal temperatures.",
